WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:04.168
- Does this sound familiar to you?

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.680
That's Mr. Krabs walking in "SpongeBob."

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.470
- Can't you just pretend to
listen for once in your life?

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:15.300
- And notice how quickly he's moving.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.880
- It's like, it's almost
like a dance, you know.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.770
- That's typical of cartoon characters,

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.590
whose exaggerated movements and behavior

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.750
call for equally exaggerated sound.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.630
(squelching)

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.910
For Foley artists like Monique or Sanaa,

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.770
this is one of the fun challenges

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.080
of creating sound for animation.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.247
And unlike live-action movies,

00:00:36.247 --> 00:00:37.172
it starts...

00:00:37.172 --> 00:00:38.848
(whooshing)

00:00:38.848 --> 00:00:39.753
...with nothing.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.844
- For live-action you
have production sound

00:00:43.844 --> 00:00:45.480
like what they recorded.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.830
So you could kind of like, be like,

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.660
"oh, this is what it sounds like."

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.170
But in animation, it's all up to you.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.870
It's like your world to
create whatever you want.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.880
- Take Gir in "Invader Zim,"

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.201
a little robot who's shaped like this.

00:00:59.201 --> 00:01:01.380
(metallic cheering)

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.150
- (Sanaa) He looks hollow.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.000
So for his body, I use this.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.946
And then for his legs, they
look like little cable legs.

00:01:10.946 --> 00:01:12.300
(clicking)

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.517
And these are his little feet.

00:01:14.517 --> 00:01:19.200
And I always had the kettle next to it

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.480
so the metal kind of resonate

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.537
and you could kind of hear his body.

00:01:23.537 --> 00:01:27.210
(metallic clicking)

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.100
- Appa, the giant, mythical beast

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.287
in "Avatar: The Last Airbender,"

00:01:31.287 --> 00:01:34.673
needed a bigger, rounder sound.

00:01:34.673 --> 00:01:37.050
(Appa grunting)

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.490
- (Sanaa) Because he's so big,

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.710
I had to try different things for him.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:45.297
First, I tried like a couple
cushions to do like massive...

00:01:45.297 --> 00:01:46.770
(thumping)

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.250
We tried big tree trunks and hit them.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.180
We couldn't really get how
big and fluffy he is as well.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.010
What I did, I got a plunger,

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.549
I got some paper napkins, filled it up

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:02.610
so it kind of took the
hollowness out of it,

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:06.300
and got multiple pieces of duct tape.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.890
When I played it, it sounded good.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.560
And I tried one that was empty.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:11.951
You could hear the difference.

00:02:13.085 --> 00:02:15.185
The duct tape muted the sound,

00:02:15.185 --> 00:02:17.040
taking away any harshness.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.110
So Appa sounds not only big,

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:20.780
but also fluffy and round

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:24.019
like the cuddly creature
he appears as onscreen.

00:02:24.019 --> 00:02:26.623
(muffled thumping)

00:02:26.623 --> 00:02:28.530
- [Katara] Seem to know everything.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.060
- [Sokka] Haha very funny.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:31.650
- Even if the characters are based

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.780
on actual real life animals,

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.600
the sounds still get wackier in animation.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.360
Like the sea creatures of "SpongeBob."

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.250
- [Monique] Krabs has a
couple things happening.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.891
Think about the crab as a shell.

00:02:42.891 --> 00:02:43.731
So steps...

00:02:43.731 --> 00:02:44.873
(rapid tapping)

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:47.012
like the sound of the crab movement...

00:02:47.012 --> 00:02:48.180
(tapping on shell)

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.520
- [Narrator] Doesn't exactly
sound like a real crab,

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:51.353
does it?

00:02:51.353 --> 00:02:53.550
(crabs scuffling)

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:56.867
That's because real crabs
don't move like this.

00:02:56.867 --> 00:02:59.820
(rapid musical tapping)

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.911
This mile-a-minute pace
isn't unique to "SpongeBob."

00:03:02.911 --> 00:03:04.327
Characters in animation
(Gir cheers)

00:03:04.327 --> 00:03:05.616
generally move faster
(metallic tapping)

00:03:05.616 --> 00:03:06.816
and more percussively
(rappid tapping)

00:03:06.816 --> 00:03:08.378
than they do in live action.

00:03:08.378 --> 00:03:09.211
(rapid tapping)

00:03:09.211 --> 00:03:12.664
Keeping up for Foley
artists can get exhausting.

00:03:12.664 --> 00:03:13.497
(laughs)

00:03:13.497 --> 00:03:15.180
- That was good.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.440
- To emphasize the force

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.110
behind all these swift jerky movements,

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.870
accent sounds take on special importance,

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:23.520
especially for fight scenes.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.980
Like the fast punches
Monique did for this scene

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.660
from the Kung Fu Panda TV show.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.432
- [Po] Heavenly club blossom.

00:03:29.432 --> 00:03:31.530
(flapping)

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.577
- Or this popping sound.

00:03:33.577 --> 00:03:35.018
(comical pop)

00:03:35.018 --> 00:03:36.930
(thumping)

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:40.776
- Anytime we have a chance
to add sound to a scene,

00:03:40.776 --> 00:03:43.525
we do, like when they're fighting.

00:03:43.525 --> 00:03:46.290
Anytime, when you see
those little white lines

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:47.532
that go (swish) like that,

00:03:47.532 --> 00:03:50.430
(slicing)

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.080
I'll add (swish) a whoosh to it.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.120
I like using bamboo sticks.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.040
You could use antennas

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.698
or anything that does this kind of sound.

00:03:58.698 --> 00:04:01.170
(swishing)

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:02.730
- Sanaa would also accent

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.551
the characters' fight moves
with what's called a "shing."

00:04:05.551 --> 00:04:10.551
(blades clashing)

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.360
- I use like a shovel.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.594
So there's like (metallic
ring) a deeper kind of shing.

00:04:14.594 --> 00:04:17.570
(shinging)

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:21.120
Also you gotta be careful
how to hold the shovel.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.490
Like you could mute it. (muted shing)

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:26.370
You could make it more ringy. (loud shing)

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:28.986
I've used a machete and a shovel.

00:04:28.986 --> 00:04:30.240
(shing)

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.550
And then I've used this thing,

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:33.750
it's not sharp at all,

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.624
with this. (shing)

00:04:36.624 --> 00:04:38.760
(clear shing)

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.400
Ooh la la. That was beautiful.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.320
- These, as with most Foley sounds,

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.060
are often combined with sound effects

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.460
added digitally by sound designers.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:51.060
- [Sanaa] So they could get
mine manipulated or have theirs.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.470
Foley and effects together.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:56.043
Just really fill it up
and make it sound great.

00:04:56.043 --> 00:04:57.059
(dog barks) (birds chirp)

00:04:57.059 --> 00:04:58.337
(zip) (cymbals crash)

00:04:58.337 --> 00:04:59.370
(zip) (cymbals crash)

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:01.830
- This combination of
Foley and digital effects

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.105
is key, especially because
there isn't any production sound

00:05:05.105 --> 00:05:06.699
to begin with.

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:09.247
It's up to the Foley artist
to fill this empty playground

00:05:09.247 --> 00:05:10.313
with sounds,

00:05:10.313 --> 00:05:13.463
each created individually
and often layered together.

00:05:13.463 --> 00:05:15.412
On "SpongeBob," for example,

00:05:15.412 --> 00:05:19.480
the Foley artists would do
something called a water path.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.150
(bubbling)

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.160
- We would take a big Tupperware tub

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.880
and fill it up until it was
about this much from the top.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:28.980
And we would do a movement.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:32.872
Instead of cloth we would move water.

00:05:32.872 --> 00:05:35.083
(water splashing)

00:05:35.083 --> 00:05:37.941
So we did all of the props dry

00:05:37.941 --> 00:05:39.960
because if we made any
of the props sound wet

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:41.670
it would be redundant with the water.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.770
- [Narrator] But Foley artists
aren't afraid to get wet

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.560
or go over the top, especially
when creating sounds

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.230
for slapstick humor and animated comedy.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:52.904
Sanaa used an extra wet shammy
to amplify the mud splats

00:05:52.904 --> 00:05:54.083
in "Rugrats."

00:05:54.083 --> 00:05:55.225
(xylophone scale)

00:05:55.225 --> 00:05:56.058
(splat)

00:05:56.058 --> 00:05:58.443
and capture the spirit
of the messy babies.

00:05:59.489 --> 00:06:00.450
(splat)

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:03.570
She also used a shammy for
the kids' eating sounds,

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:05.910
which again had to be exaggerated

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.220
to match the exaggerated visuals.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.100
- So whenever we had Rugrats eating,

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.040
again animation make it bigger than life,

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.150
you know, over the top.

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:19.530
(repeated squelching)

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.948
So for that slurping,

00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:23.280
(slurps)

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.440
- Gee they must be really hungry.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.046
- (Sanaa) I'm gonna do
an element of shammy,

00:06:28.046 --> 00:06:31.183
and I'm gonna do a bowl,
like drinking out of a bowl.

00:06:31.183 --> 00:06:32.600
(slurps noisily)

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:34.340
(swallows noisily)

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:35.310
(slurps)

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.590
- Gee they must be really hungry.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:38.786
- [Narrator] All kinds of mouth sounds

00:06:38.786 --> 00:06:40.770
tend to get heightened in animation,

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:42.990
like Appa with his gooey tongue lick.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:47.627
- We wanna get something
nice and juicy and wet.

00:06:47.627 --> 00:06:50.044
(squelching)

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.790
- [Narrator] And this
scene in "Teen Titans."

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:59.055
- Wider, I need to get
both my hands in there.

00:06:59.055 --> 00:06:59.888
(squishing)

00:06:59.888 --> 00:07:00.721
(gagging)

00:07:00.721 --> 00:07:01.662
That's one. Wider.

00:07:01.662 --> 00:07:02.730
(pained gagging)
(louder squishing)

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:04.200
There we go.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.020
- Okay. She's sticking
her hand in his mouth.

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:11.886
We're gonna layer the
sound of me using my mouth.

00:07:11.886 --> 00:07:13.977
(lips smacking)

00:07:13.977 --> 00:07:16.110
And then we're gonna layer
it with something else

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.854
that sounds cavernous and
open, which is this glove.

00:07:18.854 --> 00:07:19.687
(glove flapping)

00:07:19.687 --> 00:07:22.560
You know, it sounds like it's
got like some depth to it.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:23.895
It's not like shallow.

00:07:23.895 --> 00:07:28.895
(glove flapping)

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:30.211
- Now. Let's see.

00:07:30.211 --> 00:07:31.210
(pained gagging)
(scuffling)

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:32.043
Hmm.

00:07:32.043 --> 00:07:34.170
- [Narrator] So the
mouth sounds get wetter.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:36.300
(squelching)

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.342
The movements get faster.

00:07:38.342 --> 00:07:39.385
(rapid tapping)

00:07:39.385 --> 00:07:40.218
(swish)

00:07:40.218 --> 00:07:41.400
(shing)

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.340
And even something as mundane as footsteps

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.550
gets played up for dramatic effect.

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:48.780
(muffled thumping)

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:51.930
Nearly every sound gets
heightened in animation,

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.684
which means the creative
possibilities for Foley artists

00:07:54.684 --> 00:07:55.517
(metallic clicking)

00:07:55.517 --> 00:07:57.363
are pretty much endless.

00:08:01.197 --> 00:08:03.197
(shing)

